Ten minutes and forty-three seconds in length,<ref name=CdmaxiDef/> "Dreamlover" (Def Club Mix) is a [[dance music|dance]], pop, [[house music|house]], and [[gospel music|gospel]] reworking of the original.<ref name="w597">{{cite web | last=Joannou | first=Cliff | title=How Mariah Carey's 'Dreamlover' Changed Dance Remixes | website=[[Rolling Stone]] | date=July 27, 2023 | url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/mariah-carey-dreamlover-dance-remix-def-club-1234795597/ | access-date=August 28, 2026}}</ref> [[David Morales]] and Carey remixed the track; Morales provided its percussion and Alec Shantzis and [[Eric Kupper]] programmed the keyboards.<ref name=12vinyl/> Carey re-recorded her vocals for the track—an uncommon practice for pop remixes at the time, when it was more common to speed up the original lead vocals.<ref name="w597"/> Commentators regarded the Def Club Mix as effectively a new song, in which the original's girlishness was substituted for a more sensual tone;{{efn|Attributed to Cliff Joannou of ''[[Rolling Stone]]'',<ref name="w597"/> Tom Breihan of ''[[Stereogum]]'',<ref name="q690"/> Tim Chan in ''[[Why Mariah Carey Matters]]'' (2023),{{sfn|Chan|2023|p=40}} and Sal Cinquemani of ''[[Slant Magazine]]''.<ref name="v054">{{cite web | last1=Cinquemani | first1=Sal | last2=Henderson | first2=Eric | title=Mariah Carey's 10 Greatest Remixes | website=[[Slant Magazine]] | date=May 24, 2024 | url=https://www.slantmagazine.com/music/the-10-greatest-mariah-carey-remixes/ }}</ref> }} Chan described it as Carey reimagining herself as a ''[[femme fatale]]''.{{sfn|Chan|2023|p=40}} 

On "Dreamlover" (Def Club Mix), Carey's [[falsetto]] vocals are huskier and lower-pitched than on the original.{{sfn|Chan|2023|p=40}}<ref name="j709">{{cite news| newspaper=[[The New York Times]] | last=Sanneh | first=Kelefa | author-link=Kelefa Sanneh | title=Critic's Choice/New CD's; Disco, Alive and Dancing | date=October 20, 2003 | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2003/10/20/arts/critic-s-choice-new-cd-s-disco-alive-and-dancing.html | access-date=August 28, 2026}}</ref> The song opens with echoing [[electronic music|electronic]] beats accompanied by [[syncopated]] [[ad-lib]]s that shift in [[pitch (music)|pitch]]. About six minutes in, Morales introduces a series of [[sound effect]]s resembling explosions, after which the arrangement develops into a slower section. It then moves toward a gospel–house [[Breakdown (music)|breakdown]], which is led by Carey's whistle register. Carey's backing vocalists [[Kelly Price]], Melonie Daniels, and Shanrae Price join her nine minutes into the track.<ref name="w597"/>

