After only a partial week of airplay, "Dreamlover" entered the [[Billboard Hot 100|''Billboard'' Hot 100 Singles]] chart dated August 7, 1993, at number 40.<ref name=Top40HotSingles>{{cite magazine|title=Hot 100 Singles|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=August 7, 1993|id={{ProQuest|227031352}}|p=74}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=Hot 100 Singles Spotlight|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=August 7, 1993|first=Kevin|last=McCabe|id={{ProQuest|227026817}}|p=76}}</ref> It was the week's highest debut and Carey's fourth single to debut in the top 40.<ref name=Top40HotSingles/><ref>{{cite magazine|title=Some Enchanted Hits From the Bong?|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=August 7, 1993|p=82|first=Fred|last=Bronson|author-link=Fred Bronson|id={{ProQuest|227026817}}}}</ref> The following week, the single registered the chart's largest increase, rising to number thirteen.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Hot 100 Singles Spotlight|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=August 14, 1993|first=Kevin|last=McCabe|id={{ProQuest|227006801}}|p=77}}</ref> In its sixth week on the Hot 100, "Dreamlover" became Carey's seventh US number-one single after replacing [[UB40]]'s "[[Can't Help Falling in Love#UB40 version|Can't Help Falling in Love]]" on the chart dated September 11, 1993. ''Billboard'' predicted that it would have a lengthy run atop the Hot 100 because none of the other top-ten songs considered potential challengers had comparable airplay.<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Carey's New No. 1 Is Dressed to the 9s|id={{ProQuest|227005978}}|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=September 11, 1993|page=88|first=Fred|last=Bronson|author-link=Fred Bronson}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=Hot 100 Singles Spotlight|id={{ProQuest|227002986}}|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=September 11, 1993|page=88|first=Kevin|last=McCabe}}</ref> The song spent eight consecutive weeks at number one on the chart and was displaced by [[Meat Loaf]]'s "[[I'd Do Anything for Love (But I Won't Do That)]]".<ref>{{Cite magazine|title=Meat Loaf Suits the Public Taste|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=November 6, 1993|page=100|id={{ProQuest|226997671}}|first=Fred|last=Bronson|author-link=Fred Bronson}}</ref> "Dreamlover" remained on the Hot 100 for twenty-nine weeks and is Carey's seventh best-performing song on the chart as of 2018.<ref name="Corpuz">{{cite magazine|last=Corpuz|first=Kristin|title=Mariah Carey's Top 40 Biggest ''Billboard'' Hot 100 Hits|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=March 27, 2018|url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/mariah-carey-top-40-biggest-billboard-hot-100-hits/|url-access=subscription|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221209020503/https://www.billboard.com/pro/mariah-carey-top-40-biggest-billboard-hot-100-hits/|archive-date=December 9, 2022}}</ref><ref name="Hot100SinglesDreamlover"/> The song ranked eighth on the [[Billboard Year-End Hot 100 singles of 1993|year-end Hot 100]] chart of 1993.<ref name=yearinmusic/>

"Dreamlover" experienced success across multiple [[radio formats]] in the United States. It topped the [[contemporary hit radio]] charts published by ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'', the ''[[Gavin Report]]'', and ''[[Radio & Records]]''; the [[urban contemporary]] charts issued by the ''Gavin Report'' and ''Radio & Records''; and the [[adult contemporary]] chart distributed by ''Radio & Records''.{{efn|Cited to multiple sources:<ref name=BillboardAC/><ref name=Mainstreamairplay/><ref name=GRAC/><ref name=Top40GRAC/><ref name="R&R"/><ref name=GavinUrban/>}} The single broke the record for the most airplay in a single week, surpassing Whitney Houston's "I Will Always Love You" (1992). It reached an estimated audience of 89 million at its weekly peak in late September 1993, as measured by ''Billboard''{{'}}s [[Broadcast Data Systems]]. "Dreamlover" held the record for three years until it was surpassed by [[Donna Lewis]]'s "[[I Love You Always Forever]]" in 1996.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/press-of-atlantic-city/123235562/|title=Superstars Mariah Carey and Billy Ray Cyrus Shine on Stage|via=[[Newspapers.com]]|date=November 26, 1993|newspaper=[[Press of Atlantic City]]|first=Nicole|last=Pensiero|page=100}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|title=Atlantic's Lewis Hits BDS Heights|first=Chuck|last=Taylor|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=October 5, 1996|page=13|id={{ProQuest|227068782}}}}</ref> It became Carey's first song to be awarded a platinum [[RIAA certification|certification]] from the [[Recording Industry Association of America]], which denotes one million certified units.<ref name=riaa/><ref>{{cite magazine|title=Hot 100 Singles Spotlight|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|p=89|date=October 16, 1993|first=Kevin|last=McCabe|id={{ProQuest|227019271}}}}</ref> One of her best-selling physical singles, "Dreamlover" has sold over 935,000 units domestically as of 2014.<ref name=us_sales>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.billboard.com/pro/ask-billboard-katy-perry-regains-no-1-momentum/ |title=Ask Billboard: Katy Perry Regains No. 1 Momentum |last=Trust |first=Gary |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |publisher=[[Prometheus Global Media]] |date=February 17, 2014 |access-date=May 4, 2015 |archive-date=March 6, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140306061759/http://www.billboard.com/articles/columns/chart-beat/5908434/ask-billboard-katy-perry-regains-no-1-momentum?page=0%2C1 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In Canada, "Dreamlover" topped both the airplay-based [[RPM (magazine)|''RPM'']] singles chart and the sales-based singles chart published by ''[[The Record (magazine)|The Record]]''.<ref name="Lwin"/><ref name=canadatopsingles/> The single also reached number one on ''The Record''{{'s}} contemporary hit radio chart,<ref name="Lwin"/> and number two and six on ''RPM''{{'s}} adult contemporary and dance/urban radio charts, respectively.<ref name=ACDreamloverCanada/><ref name=DUDreamloverCanada/> In Oceania, it peaked at numbers two and seven on the respective singles charts of [[Official Aotearoa Music Charts|New Zealand]] and [[ARIA Charts|Australia]].<ref name=AUDream/><ref name=newzealand/> The [[Australian Recording Industry Association]] awarded "Dreamlover" a six-times platinum certification,<ref name=auscert/> and the [[Recording Industry Association of New Zealand]] and [[Music Canada]] both certified it gold.<ref name=NZcerts/><ref name=dreamlovercanadacert/> In Europe, the song reached number two in Portugal;<ref name=portugalchart/> numbers eight and nine on the Netherlands' [[Dutch Top 40|Top 40]] all-format chart and its [[Dutch Single Top 100|Top 100]] physical singles chart, respectively;<ref name=Dutch40/><ref name=Dutch100/> and number nine in the United Kingdom, where it became one of Carey's best-selling singles.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.officialcharts.com/charts/singles-chart/19930829/7501/|title=Top 40 Official UK Singles Archive|work=[[Official Charts Company]]|date=September 4, 1993|access-date=November 28, 2010|archive-date=November 30, 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161130112537/http://www.officialcharts.com/charts/singles-chart/19930829/7501/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="uksales">{{cite web|url=http://www.mtv.co.uk/shows/mtv-official-countdowns/episode/mariah-carey-official-top-20 |title=Mariah Carey Official Top 20 Best Selling Singles in the UK |publisher=[[MTV (UK and Ireland)|MTV]]. [[MTV Networks Europe]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110606035449/http://www.mtv.co.uk/shows/mtv-official-countdowns/episode/mariah-carey-official-top-20 |archive-date=June 6, 2011 }}</ref> It also charted in Finland, France, Germany, Iceland, Ireland, Lithuania, Sweden, and Switzerland.{{efn|Cited to multiple sources:<ref name=finnishperformance/><ref name=francedreamlover/><ref name=irishchart/><ref name=lithuania/>}}

