[[Diane Martel]] directed the music video for "Dreamlover", the first of multiple collaborations between her and Carey. The video was filmed in [[Copake, New York]] in June 1993. It depicts Carey swimming in a pool by a waterfall with her dog Jack, lying in a field of wildflowers, and singing in front of a group of hip-hop dancers and from a hot air balloon.<ref name="q690"/>{{sfn|Nickson|1998|pp=107, 119}} Chan described her outfit in the video as evoking a [[girl-next-door]] persona; she wears a black-and-white [[flannel]] shirt tied above her [[midriff]].{{sfn|Chan|2023|p=39}} The video received heavy [[Rotation (broadcast programming)|rotation]] on several music video channels in the United States.{{sfn|Nickson|1998|p=107}}<ref name="n650">{{cite web| first=Marisa | last=Sullivan |date=September 12, 2023| title=Mariah Carey Shares Photo 'Homage' to 'Dreamlover' Music Video 30 Years After Original Release | website=[[People (magazine)|People]] | url=https://people.com/mariah-carey-dreamlover-music-video-photos-homage-instagram-7968420 | access-date=September 4, 2026}}</ref> As measured by [[Broadcast Data Systems]], it topped the weekly airplay charts published by the television channels [[VH1]]<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Video Monitor|date=October 16, 1993|page=46|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|id={{ProQuest|227018623}}}}</ref> and [[BET]]<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Video Monitor|date=September 25, 1993|page=61|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|id={{ProQuest|227024321}}}}</ref> and reached the top ten on [[MTV]].<ref>{{cite magazine|title=Video Monitor|date=October 9, 1993|page=47|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|id={{ProQuest|227021410}}}}</ref>  Critics embraced the video's lighthearted nature and felt it reflected the song's bliss.{{efn|Attributed to Tom Breihan of ''[[Stereogum]]'',<ref name="q690"/> Andrew Chan in ''[[Why Mariah Carey Matters]]'',{{sfn|Chan|2023|p=39}} and [[Chris Nickson]] in ''[[Mariah Carey Revisited: Her Story]]''{{sfn|Nickson|1998|p=107}}}}

Carey performed "Dreamlover" on several occasions.{{efn|This is not a exhaustive list of her performances, only those reported by reliable sources; others may be included in [[List of Mariah Carey live performances#Televised performances|List of Mariah Carey live performances § Televised performances]].}} In 1993, she promoted it through ''[[The Arsenio Hall Show]]''; ''Rolling Stone'' writers felt that the performance was an example of her voice resonating more when conveying infatuation.<ref>{{cite web | title=15 Incredible Mariah Carey Performances | website=[[Rolling Stone]] | date=May 15, 2020 | url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/mariah-carey-performances-vision-love-butterfly-999954/dreamlover-28794/ | access-date=April 18, 2026}}</ref> That same year, she performed "Dreamlover"—alongside other songs from ''Music Box'' and some of her older songs—at New York's [[Proctor's Theatre (Schenectady, New York)|Proctor's Theatre]] in July 1993, captured in the one-hour television special ''[[Here Is Mariah Carey]]''.{{sfn|Carey|2020|p=[https://books.google.com/books?id=kePYDwAAQBAJ&pg=PT171 171]}}<ref>{{cite web | last=Arnold | first=Chad | title=When Mariah Carey, the Queen of Christmas, ruled Schenectady's Proctors | website=[[The Daily Gazette]] | date=April 15, 2026 | url=https://www.dailygazette.com/life_and_arts/mariah-carey-proctors-christmas/article_e20b77d8-a009-11ee-b94b-3fbce1e39aa0.html | access-date=April 18, 2026}}</ref> Carey promoted the song to a British audience through ''[[Top of the Pops]]'' in August 1993.<ref>{{cite web | title=Artist Profile: Mariah Carey | website=[[Top of the Pops]] | url=https://totparchive.co.uk/artist.php?name=mariah-carey | access-date=April 15, 2026}}</ref> In October 1995, she performed "Dreamlover" during a concert at [[Madison Square Garden]] in New York City.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-atlanta-constitution/205395421/|title=Special Ushers in 'Grinch' Time Again|newspaper=[[The Atlanta Constitution]]|date=November 26, 1995|page=343|via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-spokesman-review/205395491/|title=Mariah Carey Feeling More in Control|newspaper=[[The Boston Globe]]|date=October 6, 1995|via=[[Newspapers.com]]|first=Steve|last=Morse}}</ref> It was included on her video album ''[[Fantasy: Mariah Carey at Madison Square Garden]]'' in 1996.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mtv.com/bands/az/carey_mariah/27983/album.jhtml|title=''Mariah Carey at Madison Square Garden''|publisher=[[MTV]]|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020601193733/http://www.mtv.com/bands/az/carey_mariah/27983/album.jhtml|archive-date=June 1, 2002}}</ref> In December 1999, she filmed ''The Mariah Carey Homecoming Special'' at her former high school in [[Huntington, New York]], to promote her album ''[[Rainbow (Mariah Carey album)|Rainbow]]''; the special included a performance of "Dreamlover".<ref>{{cite news|title=Best Bets This Week|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-times-transcript/205395761/|date=December 10, 1999|newspaper=[[Times & Transcript]]|via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref>

"Dreamlover" became a staple of Carey's [[concert tour]]s; she performed it during her [[Music Box Tour]] in 1993<ref>{{cite news|title=Mariah Carey Sheds Shyness in Spectacular Centrum Show|newspaper=[[The Boston Globe]]|date=November 10, 1993|page=58|first=Steve|last=Morse|id={{ProQuest|294817548}}}}</ref> and [[Daydream World Tour]] in 1996.{{sfn|Nickson|1998|p=155}} Her performance of the song at the [[Tokyo Dome]] during the latter tour is included on her compilation album ''[[The Rarities (Mariah Carey album)|The Rarities]]'' (2020).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.oricon.co.jp/prof/163336/products/1368779/1/|title=レアリティーズ|trans-title=''Rarities''|language=ja|publisher=[[Oricon]]|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221119214508/https://www.oricon.co.jp/prof/163336/products/1368779/1/|archive-date=November 19, 2022}}</ref>  She continued to sing "Dreamlover" on most of her following world tours, including the [[Butterfly World Tour]] (1998),<ref>{{cite news|title=Carey Needs More Song, Less Style|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/honolulu-star-advertiser/114283185/|first=Deb|last=Aoki|date=February 22, 1998|p=24|newspaper=[[Honolulu Star-Advertiser]]|via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> [[Rainbow World Tour]] (2000),<ref>{{cite news|title=Mariah Carey's Canadian Debut Concert Thrills Fans|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-tribune/205399794/|date=April 20, 2000|p=18|newspaper=[[Welland Tribune]]|via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> [[Charmbracelet World Tour]] (2003–2004),<ref>{{cite news|title=Mariah Spotlights Her Recovery, Wardrobe|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/arizona-daily-star/125921825/|first=Anthony|last=Broadman|date=December 21, 2003|p=59|newspaper=[[Arizona Daily Star]]|via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> [[Adventures of Mimi]] (2006),<ref>{{cite news|title=A Little Too Much|url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-toronto-sun/205400335/|first=Steve|last=Tilley|date=August 14, 2006|p=18|newspaper=[[The Toronto Sun]]|via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> and [[Caution World Tour]] (2019).<ref name="n934">{{cite web | last=Cragg | first=Michael | title=Mariah Carey Review – Gold-Plated Pop Diva Cements Her Legacy | website=[[The Guardian]] | date=May 26, 2019 | url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2019/may/26/mariah-carey-review-royal-albert-hall-london | access-date=September 6, 2026}}</ref> Carey has performed the song for two of her [[concert residencies]] in Las Vegas, [[Number 1 to Infinity (concert residency)|#1 to Infinity]] (2015–2017) and [[the Celebration of Mimi]] (2024–2025).<ref>{{cite news|date=May 7, 2015|title=Review: Mariah Carey and Her Can't-Look-Away Debut in Las Vegas|last=Caramanica|first=Jon|author-link=Jon Caramanica|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2015/05/08/arts/music/review-mariah-carey-and-her-cant-look-away-debut-in-las-vegas.html|url-access=limited|newspaper=[[The New York Times]]|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220514094436/https://www.nytimes.com/2015/05/08/arts/music/review-mariah-carey-and-her-cant-look-away-debut-in-las-vegas.html|archive-date=May 14, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|last1=Gray|first1=Mark|last2=Sheeler|first2=Jason|url=https://people.com/mariah-carey-kicks-off-spectacular-new-las-vegas-residency-im-feeling-good-8631180|title=Mariah Carey Kicks Off Spectacular New Las Vegas Residency: 'I'm Feeling Good'|magazine=[[People (magazine)|People]]|date=April 13, 2024|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240414091406/https://people.com/mariah-carey-kicks-off-spectacular-new-las-vegas-residency-im-feeling-good-8631180|archive-date=April 14, 2024}}</ref>

